{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 33, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "שבעה", :translit "shevah", :gloss "Shevah [the well's name]"} {:he "על כן ככולם כתיכ שמה או שם", :translit "al ken k'khulam khativ shemah o shem", :gloss "therefore in all cases it is written 'its name' or 'a name'"} {:he "ויקרא אותה שבעה", :translit "vayikra otah shevah", :gloss "and he called it Shevah"} {:he "ולא כתיב שם", :translit "v'lo khativ shem", :gloss "and 'name' is not written"} {:he "קטן נתתיך בגוים", :translit "katan n'tatikha bagoyim", :gloss "small I have made you among the nations"} {:he "ולא שם לו על פני חוץ", :translit "v'lo shem lo al pnei chotz", :gloss "and not 'a name for it on the surface'"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Masoretic contrast: the expected word שם is absent at Genesis 26:33, signaling Esau/Edom's diminished status."} {:kind :other, :note "Gezarah shavah linking the missing שם at Genesis 26:33 to Obadiah 1:2, which prophesies Edom's reduction."}], :frame {:text "The phrase קטן נתתיך בגוים appears at Obadiah 1:2 as a divine oracle against Edom. Genesis 36:8 identifies Esau with Edom and Seir, the nation that descended from Isaac's son. The Ba'al HaTurim reads the anomalous spelling at Genesis 26:33—where the well-naming verse omits the word שם that normally appears in such formulas—as a literary allusion to Obadiah's prophecy that Edom would be made insignificant among the nations.", :sources ["Genesis 36:8" "Obadiah 1:2"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :other, :note "Masoretic contrast: the expected word שם is absent at Genesis 26:33, signaling Esau/Edom's diminished status.", :by :selah, :ran? false} {:kind :other, :note "Gezarah shavah linking the missing שם at Genesis 26:33 to Obadiah 1:2, which prophesies Edom's reduction.", :by :selah, :ran? false}], :book :genesis, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 26, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:08:29.516580189Z", :model "glm-5.2"}